            <ArticleTitle>Novel arylazoarylmethane as potential inhibitor of macrophage migration inhibitory factor.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>104-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1002/ardp.201300243</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Macrophage migration inhibitory factor (MIF), a cytokine involved in the pathogenesis of sepsis, diabetes, asthma, arthritis, and cancer, has an enzymatic active site that has proven to be an effective target for small molecule-based inhibitors. Herein, we describe the synthesis of a novel arylazoarylmethane compound (3) from a known arylhydrazone MIF inhibitor (2). This new compound has improved stability and in vivo biological activity.</AbstractText>
